WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.410
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.366 --> 00:00:08.240
SAPTARSHI MUKHERJEE:
I'm Saptarshi Mukherjee.

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:11.060
I lead the product marketing
function for our data analytics

00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:12.874
and IoT business areas.

00:00:12.874 --> 00:00:14.790
And today we're going
to have a really, really

00:00:14.790 --> 00:00:16.320
interesting topic.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:20.190
We're going to focus on the
total economic impact of moving

00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:21.840
data analytics
workloads to cloud.

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:24.390
How do you evaluate
some of the options?

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:26.130
We will be joined
by Mike Leone--

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:29.790
he's a senior analyst from ESG,
Enterprise Strategy Group--

00:00:29.790 --> 00:00:31.530
and Sam King who
is an engineering

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:33.330
manager from Nuna Health.

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:36.480
And they're going to share
some of their insights.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:39.810
So why did we choose
this particular topic?

00:00:39.810 --> 00:00:42.870
Google Cloud is a fairly
deep technical conference.

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:46.460
But when we engage with
customers, a lot of them,

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:48.540
one of the common
recurring themes

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:51.590
that we hear from them,
that we have the best

00:00:51.590 --> 00:00:54.120
technologies in our
on-premise data centers, or on

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:55.650
cloud x or y.

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:57.720
If we move to Google
Cloud, how is it

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:02.820
going to impact our cost,
our total economic value?

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:05.129
So it's important
for us to understand

00:01:05.129 --> 00:01:08.430
that all data warehouses
that are running in the cloud

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:09.350
are not the same.

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:11.490
All Hadoop or Spark
workloads that

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:13.540
could run on different
clouds are not the same.

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:16.050
So how do we essentially
evaluate those options

00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:18.750
through some fine
grained parameters

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:22.080
and come up with a
TCO model that's going

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:24.130
to give you a long term value?

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:26.670
So today we're going
to talk about why

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:29.760
economic impact in the cloud,
especially for data analytics,

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:32.640
matters, some of the
key considerations

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:35.230
for evaluating the
economic impact,

00:01:35.230 --> 00:01:38.200
and then we will take two
use cases, one moving data

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:39.426
warehouses to the cloud.

00:01:39.426 --> 00:01:41.800
And we will talk about moving
data warehouse to BigQuery,

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:42.840
as an example.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:45.660
And Mike is going to take us
through the economic rationale

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:47.380
and economic modeling for that.

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:49.380
And then we're going
to talk to Sam.

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:52.470
They have actually moved their
on-premise data management

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:54.120
platform to Google Cloud.

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:56.280
What were some of their
decision criteria?

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:58.140
What has been their
experience so far?

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:00.480
And then we're going to
go to the next use case,

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:03.030
talking Hadoop and
Spark movement to cloud,

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:06.240
and how to evaluate the economic
impact for that particular one,

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:07.650
and then come to
some conclusion.

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:10.449
Hopefully it will be a useful
session for all of you.

00:02:10.449 --> 00:02:13.380
So with that, I'll first hand
over the presentation to Mike

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:16.030
to talk about why cloud
matters and why data analytics.

00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:16.530
Mike.

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:19.800
MIKE LEONE: Thanks, Sap.

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:20.716
How's everybody doing?

00:02:20.716 --> 00:02:23.190
Exciting first day?

00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:26.220
So Enterprise Strategy Group
is an analyst and strategy firm

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:29.340
anchored in end user market
research and validation

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:30.450
services.

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:33.090
So we conduct annual
research throughout the year

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:35.250
across various
segments within IT.

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:37.766
And one of our most
popular surveys

00:02:37.766 --> 00:02:39.390
is our IT spending
and tensions survey.

00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:41.910
That's where we ask mid-market
and enterprise class

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:44.604
organizations where
they're placing their bets.

00:02:44.604 --> 00:02:46.020
What are they
spending their money

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:48.700
on over the next
12 to 18 months?

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:51.630
And within that survey, we
track technology adoption,

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:53.910
challenges, pain
points, et cetera.

00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:56.520
And obviously, one of the
major trends is around cloud.

00:02:56.520 --> 00:03:01.110
And this year the surveys
show that 85% of organizations

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:04.210
are leveraging cloud computing
services to some degree.

00:03:04.210 --> 00:03:07.050
Now of course, there are three
buckets of consuming cloud

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:08.730
computing services.

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:10.950
Software-as-a-service
is in the lead,

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:13.890
but the big takeaway here
is platform-as-a-service is

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:14.820
gaining traction.

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:17.280
Organizations are
starting to realize

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:19.920
the benefits of being able
to abstract the hardware

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:23.500
and focus on the data
and on the applications.

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:25.260
So with that in mind,
I'm sure many of you

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:27.626
have already migrated
to the cloud.

00:03:27.626 --> 00:03:29.250
We leveraged our
research to understand

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:31.541
what are some of the advantages
of moving to the cloud.

00:03:31.541 --> 00:03:32.430
And this is general.

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:33.870
This isn't specific
to analytics,

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:36.706
and far and away, it's
cost savings, right.

00:03:36.706 --> 00:03:38.580
And of course, there's
a bunch of other items

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:40.410
that we can talk about.

00:03:40.410 --> 00:03:42.630
Here are the additional
items-- flexibility,

00:03:42.630 --> 00:03:46.500
accessibility, reliability,
manageability and whatnot.

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:49.500
But the big thing to think
about is all of these items

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:51.300
can come back to cost.

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:54.360
Accessibility, how is your
cost or revenue impacted

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:58.450
if there's downtime, there's
a failure with reliability?

00:03:58.450 --> 00:04:01.150
OpEx is a big thing that
we're going to focus on.

00:04:01.150 --> 00:04:04.650
So now let's shift to use cases.

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:06.600
Far and away, the major
use case right now

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:11.880
is lifting shift with
IaaS and PaaS consumers.

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:14.460
The next use case on
the list is leveraging

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:17.579
public cloud computing
services for analytics.

00:04:17.579 --> 00:04:21.180
So think data warehousing,
data processing, BI, et cetera.

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:23.225
And I found that
really interesting.

00:04:23.225 --> 00:04:24.600
The reason it's
interesting to me

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:27.660
is this was a more
popular use case

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:30.030
than leveraging the cloud
as a cold storage tier,

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:33.120
as a backup target
for archive, for DR.

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:36.100
So I thought that that
was pretty impressive.

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:38.740
And now let's talk a
little about spend.

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:40.740
What are mid-market and
enterprise organizations

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:43.870
spending on over the
next 12 to 18 months?

00:04:43.870 --> 00:04:46.112
And the interesting
piece here is

00:04:46.112 --> 00:04:47.820
there's an interesting
marriage, I think,

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:48.940
between these two items.

00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:51.870
So over 50% of organizations
are going to spend more

00:04:51.870 --> 00:04:53.070
on cloud computing services.

00:04:53.070 --> 00:04:56.370
They're also going to spend
more on data analytics solutions

00:04:56.370 --> 00:04:59.080
and becoming more data driven.

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:00.729
So when you put
the two together,

00:05:00.729 --> 00:05:03.270
organizations aren't just going
to take out their checkbooks,

00:05:03.270 --> 00:05:04.500
and say, yeah, I
need these things.

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:05.625
Everybody else is doing it.

00:05:05.625 --> 00:05:07.800
I need to remain competitive.

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:09.270
They need to evaluate the costs.

00:05:09.270 --> 00:05:12.240
And when you're evaluating cost,
especially going from on-prem

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:16.180
to the cloud, you need to think
about a little differently.

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:18.150
So I'm going to
share with you what

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:20.580
I think is a good high level
framework where you can start

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:23.190
to map out how
those costs differ,

00:05:23.190 --> 00:05:25.590
specifically when
you're moving analytics

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:27.700
from on-prem to the cloud.

00:05:27.700 --> 00:05:31.584
So the first bucket is
really traditional CapEx.

00:05:31.584 --> 00:05:33.000
But to make it
fair for the cloud,

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:35.880
we have to account for if
you're leveraging a cloud

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:38.280
service where you're
paying an upfront cost.

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:40.200
So think of this
as a cost that you

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:42.600
have to pay before you're
starting to realize value.

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:44.910
So I bought the solution,
but it's sitting idle.

00:05:44.910 --> 00:05:46.950
I'm still working on
configuring, deploying,

00:05:46.950 --> 00:05:48.930
testing, et cetera.

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:52.200
The next, to really
account for the fact

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:54.870
that you're going to have
monthly costs associated

00:05:54.870 --> 00:05:57.840
with consuming
cloud resources, we

00:05:57.840 --> 00:05:59.850
have another category
around just cloud costs,

00:05:59.850 --> 00:06:02.250
so month to month
costs associated

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:05.760
with storage, compute,
streaming, ingest, et cetera.

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:08.010
Now is where I think it gets
a little interesting when

00:06:08.010 --> 00:06:10.010
we shift from CapEx to OpEx.

00:06:10.010 --> 00:06:14.340
And this is really where
I feel the cloud shines.

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:17.410
So when we bucketize OpEx,
we do it in two ways.

00:06:17.410 --> 00:06:20.160
First, it's just
traditional OpEx,

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:23.760
so that's time
and manpower spent

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:26.720
dealing with deployment,
configuration, procurement, et

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:27.780
cetera.

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:30.840
This isn't necessarily focused
on the actual application

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:32.161
or the data.

00:06:32.161 --> 00:06:33.660
That's what the
next category's for,

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:36.270
which is around the
administration costs.

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:39.510
So a good way to think about
the administration cost--

00:06:39.510 --> 00:06:41.670
obviously, I have
it here around what

00:06:41.670 --> 00:06:43.560
you're doing on a daily basis.

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:47.730
But it's really more
about an IT administrator

00:06:47.730 --> 00:06:50.940
and the amount of time they
spend responding to end users.

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:52.410
So if you're an
IT administrator,

00:06:52.410 --> 00:06:55.170
and you're serving as a
gatekeeper to some degree--

00:06:55.170 --> 00:06:57.382
the example here is around
query orchestration--

00:06:57.382 --> 00:06:59.340
if you need to prevent
end users from accessing

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:02.190
the system in an
unlimited way, there's

00:07:02.190 --> 00:07:05.210
a cost that's going to
be associated with that.

00:07:05.210 --> 00:07:07.920
Finally, maybe the most
straightforward here,

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:08.860
maintenance costs.

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:11.800
So think support, professional
services, et cetera.

00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:15.800
So to boil it up,
when we think TCO,

00:07:15.800 --> 00:07:17.300
it really requires
a different frame

00:07:17.300 --> 00:07:18.716
of thinking when
you're leveraging

00:07:18.716 --> 00:07:20.230
the cloud for data analytics.

00:07:20.230 --> 00:07:21.490
It's not just CapEx.

00:07:21.490 --> 00:07:23.710
If anything, it's really
about understanding the OpEx

00:07:23.710 --> 00:07:24.760
behind it.

00:07:24.760 --> 00:07:27.220
So first and foremost,
you're really

00:07:27.220 --> 00:07:30.160
overcoming those
traditional cost burdens.

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:33.035
You don't have to worry about
configuration, deployment.

00:07:33.035 --> 00:07:35.410
You don't have to worry about
over-provisioning resources

00:07:35.410 --> 00:07:38.520
if you're leveraging the
right cloud platform.

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:42.680
And simplicity is a big
deal for a couple reasons.

00:07:42.680 --> 00:07:46.890
First, it's about empowering
the IT generalist.

00:07:46.890 --> 00:07:49.920
You don't really have to
worry about that skilled admin

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:54.140
dedicating a ton of their time
to deal with end user requests

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:55.890
when you're leveraging
the cloud platform,

00:07:55.890 --> 00:07:58.440
and that simplicity
extends to the business.

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:01.590
If you have the right system
that's easy to use, intuitive,

00:08:01.590 --> 00:08:04.260
you can really allow
the business analyst

00:08:04.260 --> 00:08:06.420
to take matters into
their own hands,

00:08:06.420 --> 00:08:08.270
in a controlled
environment, right.

00:08:08.270 --> 00:08:10.270
We don't want to give
them access to everything.

00:08:10.270 --> 00:08:12.180
And with that
simplicity, it actually

00:08:12.180 --> 00:08:16.090
extends for the IT admin
around cost transparency,

00:08:16.090 --> 00:08:19.120
visibility into what
those users are doing.

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:22.480
And that's a pretty
big deal, and it

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:24.730
yields some pretty
significant cost savings.

00:08:24.730 --> 00:08:28.440
Now, the first three items
I talked about here, they're

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:30.870
obviously clear positives
for the cloud, right.

00:08:30.870 --> 00:08:32.828
Yeah, great, we're going
to save a ton of money

00:08:32.828 --> 00:08:33.940
going on the cloud.

00:08:33.940 --> 00:08:35.070
There's going to be some
additional costs you have

00:08:35.070 --> 00:08:36.150
to think about,
and one of those is

00:08:36.150 --> 00:08:38.220
if you're leveraging
streaming applications.

00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:41.400
When you're not necessarily
migrating in bulk,

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:43.799
but when you're
ingesting on a day

00:08:43.799 --> 00:08:47.239
to day basis or a monthly basis,
you're going to incur costs.

00:08:47.239 --> 00:08:48.780
The good news is
that they make tools

00:08:48.780 --> 00:08:52.580
available to ensure that it's
reliable, highly available,

00:08:52.580 --> 00:08:55.260
the workflows are
sustainable, et cetera.

00:08:55.260 --> 00:08:59.190
So a lot of those items
can be quantified.

00:08:59.190 --> 00:09:02.100
You can put a dollar value
down that's associated

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:03.840
with all those things.

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:06.495
Where organizations
tend not to look

00:09:06.495 --> 00:09:08.520
is around the opportunity cost.

00:09:08.520 --> 00:09:12.614
And there's a lot of
angles to opportunity cost,

00:09:12.614 --> 00:09:15.030
but what I would say is, there's
so many organizations now

00:09:15.030 --> 00:09:17.654
that are data organizations, if
not every organization's a data

00:09:17.654 --> 00:09:18.439
organization.

00:09:18.439 --> 00:09:20.730
They understand that data is
their most valuable asset.

00:09:20.730 --> 00:09:22.632
They want to be
more data driven.

00:09:22.632 --> 00:09:24.090
They want to leverage
a system that

00:09:24.090 --> 00:09:28.380
can respond to the business in
real time or near real time.

00:09:28.380 --> 00:09:31.560
And they want to make sure
that that system allows

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:36.270
for end users and IT admins to
do more for the organization.

00:09:36.270 --> 00:09:39.300
They want everybody focusing
on that core business.

00:09:39.300 --> 00:09:42.830
With that in mind, if
you're allowed or enabled

00:09:42.830 --> 00:09:46.680
by the platform you're using to
let more end users, analysts,

00:09:46.680 --> 00:09:49.937
leverage a system to
try and derive insight,

00:09:49.937 --> 00:09:51.520
you're going to
increase collaboration

00:09:51.520 --> 00:09:53.500
across the entire organization.

00:09:53.500 --> 00:09:55.120
And that's a pretty big deal.

00:09:55.120 --> 00:09:58.030
So with that in mind,
I'm going to shift back

00:09:58.030 --> 00:10:01.177
to Sap, who is going to
talk about two use cases

00:10:01.177 --> 00:10:02.260
that he mentioned earlier.

00:10:02.260 --> 00:10:04.301
First, we're going to be
chatting about BigQuery.

00:10:06.587 --> 00:10:08.170
SAPTARSHI MUKHERJEE:
Yeah, absolutely.

00:10:08.170 --> 00:10:08.890
Thanks, Mike.

00:10:08.890 --> 00:10:09.820
So let's make it real.

00:10:09.820 --> 00:10:13.180
I think we talked about
some of the frameworks

00:10:13.180 --> 00:10:16.060
to evaluate costs,
both CapEx and OpEx,

00:10:16.060 --> 00:10:18.590
but we're going to look
at two specific use cases.

00:10:18.590 --> 00:10:21.460
One is moving your data
warehouse to BigQuery.

00:10:21.460 --> 00:10:24.320
Let's try to quantify
some of these benefits.

00:10:24.320 --> 00:10:27.190
So how many of you are
aware of what's BigQuery?

00:10:27.190 --> 00:10:28.630
Quick show of hands.

00:10:28.630 --> 00:10:29.590
Wow.

00:10:29.590 --> 00:10:30.490
We all love BigQuery.

00:10:30.490 --> 00:10:31.364
It's a great product.

00:10:31.364 --> 00:10:35.650
So BigQuery is Google Cloud's
native data warehouse system.

00:10:35.650 --> 00:10:37.750
It's a serverless
data warehouse that

00:10:37.750 --> 00:10:40.660
doesn't require you to
preconfigure nodes and clusters

00:10:40.660 --> 00:10:42.070
or do capacity planning.

00:10:42.070 --> 00:10:46.360
You can get your data in, write
SQL queries, get your answers.

00:10:46.360 --> 00:10:47.600
It's very simple.

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:48.840
Let's keep it that way.

00:10:48.840 --> 00:10:50.650
There are a lot of
benefits of BigQuery,

00:10:50.650 --> 00:10:54.790
like it's highly durable,
and encrypted, secure.

00:10:54.790 --> 00:10:57.580
It allows real time
streaming data analytics.

00:10:57.580 --> 00:11:01.780
But we will try to see
how all of these benefits

00:11:01.780 --> 00:11:04.942
play a role in terms of the TCO.

00:11:04.942 --> 00:11:06.400
So this is BigQuery's
architecture.

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:09.370
So it's fundamentally
different from traditional data

00:11:09.370 --> 00:11:12.430
warehouses in a way, that
BigQuery does decoupling

00:11:12.430 --> 00:11:13.710
of storage and compute.

00:11:13.710 --> 00:11:17.350
So what it means is when you
get your data inside BigQuery,

00:11:17.350 --> 00:11:21.970
you can store your data in a
replicated, highly distributed,

00:11:21.970 --> 00:11:25.480
and consistent storage system
with very high reliability

00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:26.680
and durability.

00:11:26.680 --> 00:11:30.190
And when you fire a query to
get an answer from your data,

00:11:30.190 --> 00:11:32.470
only then, Google
dynamically previsions

00:11:32.470 --> 00:11:34.870
compute infrastructure
to query the data

00:11:34.870 --> 00:11:36.730
and get you the
insights that you need.

00:11:36.730 --> 00:11:41.230
So your compute infrastructure,
which is very, very costly,

00:11:41.230 --> 00:11:44.120
is not running all the time,
unlike many other cloud

00:11:44.120 --> 00:11:46.120
based data warehousing system.

00:11:46.120 --> 00:11:48.490
And then there
are other benefits

00:11:48.490 --> 00:11:51.320
that we're going to talk
about in this context.

00:11:51.320 --> 00:11:53.830
So the first thing is, in a
traditional data warehouse--

00:11:53.830 --> 00:11:56.070
those of us who have
operated in traditional data

00:11:56.070 --> 00:11:59.630
warehouse-- you typically
load your data overnight.

00:11:59.630 --> 00:12:02.380
Next day morning, you will
get this management reports

00:12:02.380 --> 00:12:03.720
based on the historical data.

00:12:03.720 --> 00:12:07.570
You do a lot of BI and
descriptive analytics

00:12:07.570 --> 00:12:08.610
on top of your data.

00:12:08.610 --> 00:12:10.540
You use traditional ETL tools.

00:12:10.540 --> 00:12:12.020
You have a DBA.

00:12:12.020 --> 00:12:14.770
The person is doing
defragmentation on disk,

00:12:14.770 --> 00:12:18.400
creating indexes, refactoring
some of the storage systems.

00:12:18.400 --> 00:12:20.620
You need to be putting
a lot of operations

00:12:20.620 --> 00:12:23.320
over it to make your
data warehouse responsive

00:12:23.320 --> 00:12:25.810
and keep the data
warehouse up and running.

00:12:25.810 --> 00:12:29.200
But BigQuery changes a lot
of those traditional mold

00:12:29.200 --> 00:12:31.030
around data warehousing, right.

00:12:31.030 --> 00:12:33.444
So we have automated
data delivery.

00:12:33.444 --> 00:12:34.860
Unlike traditional
data warehouse,

00:12:34.860 --> 00:12:36.610
and you own the data
warehouse on-premise,

00:12:36.610 --> 00:12:37.977
you get like, an empty box.

00:12:37.977 --> 00:12:40.060
And you need to spend three
months to get the data

00:12:40.060 --> 00:12:41.110
warehouse up and running.

00:12:41.110 --> 00:12:43.450
In some of the other
clouds also, what you get

00:12:43.450 --> 00:12:45.850
is a bunch of
nodes and clusters,

00:12:45.850 --> 00:12:49.090
where in BigQuery, we have
connectors for direct SaaS

00:12:49.090 --> 00:12:50.940
application, like
AdWords and YouTube,

00:12:50.940 --> 00:12:53.860
so you can immediately
start getting data

00:12:53.860 --> 00:12:56.560
from those applications
directly into BigQuery.

00:12:56.560 --> 00:12:59.640
BigQuery also lays the
foundation for AI, not just BI.

00:12:59.640 --> 00:13:01.690
We do support a lot
of BI and reporting

00:13:01.690 --> 00:13:04.660
on top of data stored in
BigQuery with the partner

00:13:04.660 --> 00:13:07.880
tools like [INAUDIBLE] and
Tableaus of the world, and also

00:13:07.880 --> 00:13:09.640
as well as with
Google Data Studio.

00:13:09.640 --> 00:13:11.860
But we also support AI.

00:13:11.860 --> 00:13:13.975
And TensorFlow has
direct connectivity.

00:13:13.975 --> 00:13:15.850
You will hear some of
the newer announcements

00:13:15.850 --> 00:13:17.530
we are going to
make in this area.

00:13:17.530 --> 00:13:20.270
BigQuery also supports real
time streaming data on-site.

00:13:20.270 --> 00:13:23.380
So that means you can actually
start streaming real time

00:13:23.380 --> 00:13:26.980
data directly inside BigQuery
and start populating the data

00:13:26.980 --> 00:13:28.630
warehouse with real time data.

00:13:28.630 --> 00:13:31.430
And then a lot of
data operations,

00:13:31.430 --> 00:13:34.540
there is no concept of
indexing, or essentially taking

00:13:34.540 --> 00:13:35.570
backups, et cetera.

00:13:35.570 --> 00:13:37.450
It's all of those data
operations steps have

00:13:37.450 --> 00:13:38.620
been automated.

00:13:38.620 --> 00:13:42.160
So if we have to translate all
of this and quantify, so what?

00:13:42.160 --> 00:13:44.560
Does it impact my TCO?

00:13:44.560 --> 00:13:45.130
It does.

00:13:45.130 --> 00:13:48.880
And we actually had ESG, and
then ESG actually conducted

00:13:48.880 --> 00:13:51.895
a study to understand if
BigQuery's architecturally

00:13:51.895 --> 00:13:54.370
a very, very different
data warehouse,

00:13:54.370 --> 00:13:56.544
how does it actually
impact customers' TCO?

00:13:56.544 --> 00:13:58.210
So Mike, what were
some of the findings?

00:14:01.060 --> 00:14:02.870
MIKE LEONE: Thanks, Sap.

00:14:02.870 --> 00:14:05.410
So as Sap mentioned, we
conducted a three year TCO

00:14:05.410 --> 00:14:06.610
analysis.

00:14:06.610 --> 00:14:09.670
And we compared on-premises
Hadoop an Amazon Redshift

00:14:09.670 --> 00:14:10.872
to Google BigQuery.

00:14:10.872 --> 00:14:12.580
And one of the things
I want to call out,

00:14:12.580 --> 00:14:15.820
very important to call out,
we looked at Amazon Redshift

00:14:15.820 --> 00:14:18.520
reserved instance pricing
and we compared it

00:14:18.520 --> 00:14:20.440
to BigQuery's on-demand pricing.

00:14:20.440 --> 00:14:22.273
You're probably saying,
well, wait a minute.

00:14:22.273 --> 00:14:23.020
That's not fair.

00:14:23.020 --> 00:14:24.394
And really, one
of the reasons we

00:14:24.394 --> 00:14:26.830
did that is because when
we were talking to some

00:14:26.830 --> 00:14:30.650
of the customers, it ended
up that leveraging on-demand

00:14:30.650 --> 00:14:32.650
was more cost effective
for them than leveraging

00:14:32.650 --> 00:14:33.500
reserved instance.

00:14:33.500 --> 00:14:35.458
Now I understand the
predictability side of it,

00:14:35.458 --> 00:14:38.080
and that's fine, and everybody
can have their views.

00:14:38.080 --> 00:14:40.120
So we took that
approach based on what

00:14:40.120 --> 00:14:41.940
we were hearing from customers.

00:14:41.940 --> 00:14:44.260
We leveraged end
user market research.

00:14:44.260 --> 00:14:47.530
We leveraged publicly available
pricing, which is fantastic

00:14:47.530 --> 00:14:48.220
about the cloud.

00:14:48.220 --> 00:14:49.803
And then we did
qualitative interviews

00:14:49.803 --> 00:14:52.150
with a number of Google
BigQuery customers,

00:14:52.150 --> 00:14:54.825
to understand where they
were and where they are now.

00:14:54.825 --> 00:14:56.200
What kind of pain
points did they

00:14:56.200 --> 00:14:57.910
have with their
previous solution,

00:14:57.910 --> 00:14:59.740
and what benefits
have they gained

00:14:59.740 --> 00:15:01.300
since leveraging BigQuery?

00:15:01.300 --> 00:15:03.580
And those customers came
from Hadoop to BigQuery.

00:15:03.580 --> 00:15:05.110
They came from
Redshift to BigQuery.

00:15:05.110 --> 00:15:07.195
They actually came from
Redshift back to on-prem,

00:15:07.195 --> 00:15:09.860
back to BigQuery, which I
thought was pretty interesting.

00:15:09.860 --> 00:15:13.210
So we took all that data,
and we created a model.

00:15:13.210 --> 00:15:16.975
And that model was spread across
three different organizations

00:15:16.975 --> 00:15:20.350
that we just kind of threw out
there as good starting points--

00:15:20.350 --> 00:15:22.502
small, medium, and
large organization.

00:15:22.502 --> 00:15:23.960
Now some of the
considerations when

00:15:23.960 --> 00:15:26.530
you're looking at an
enterprise data warehouse,

00:15:26.530 --> 00:15:29.372
we factored in, number of
data citizens, analysts

00:15:29.372 --> 00:15:31.580
that are actually doing the
querying, how much data's

00:15:31.580 --> 00:15:36.367
stored, how much data is queried
of the data that's stored,

00:15:36.367 --> 00:15:38.200
and then factored in
streaming applications,

00:15:38.200 --> 00:15:40.930
because you're going to
incur additional costs.

00:15:40.930 --> 00:15:44.050
Now I know these three year
savings are pretty eye popping.

00:15:44.050 --> 00:15:45.659
You don't really expect it.

00:15:45.659 --> 00:15:47.200
So I can't just show
you the numbers.

00:15:47.200 --> 00:15:49.090
I got to show you a little bit
what goes behind the numbers,

00:15:49.090 --> 00:15:50.020
right.

00:15:50.020 --> 00:15:51.960
So we actually went into--

00:15:51.960 --> 00:15:55.520
I know this isn't [? iChart. ?]
This is one of many Sheets.

00:15:55.520 --> 00:15:57.710
And this is really
boiling it up.

00:15:57.710 --> 00:16:01.070
But the important takeaway
is, you know the buckets

00:16:01.070 --> 00:16:02.520
I mentioned earlier?

00:16:02.520 --> 00:16:04.270
We started with those
buckets, and then we

00:16:04.270 --> 00:16:06.070
added line items, one
underneath, underneath,

00:16:06.070 --> 00:16:07.510
underneath, and started
factoring in costs,

00:16:07.510 --> 00:16:09.910
and taking all the information
that was available,

00:16:09.910 --> 00:16:11.890
and applying it to the
three organizations

00:16:11.890 --> 00:16:13.210
that we were modeling.

00:16:13.210 --> 00:16:15.760
And one of the
takeaways here is,

00:16:15.760 --> 00:16:18.230
while BigQuery
provided savings or was

00:16:18.230 --> 00:16:20.230
at least equal to Redshift
in a number of cases,

00:16:20.230 --> 00:16:23.030
where it really shined
was on the OpEx side.

00:16:23.030 --> 00:16:26.290
So you can see here about,
I want to say, three

00:16:26.290 --> 00:16:28.420
or four buckets
down, you're going

00:16:28.420 --> 00:16:30.050
to see significant
savings on BigQuery.

00:16:30.050 --> 00:16:30.675
So why is that?

00:16:30.675 --> 00:16:33.050
Well, BigQuery markets itself
as a PaaS and it is a PaaS.

00:16:33.050 --> 00:16:33.737
It's serverless.

00:16:33.737 --> 00:16:36.070
You don't have to worry about
the infrastructure at all.

00:16:36.070 --> 00:16:37.890
That takes a massive
amount of savings

00:16:37.890 --> 00:16:41.551
out from Redshift,
which you still

00:16:41.551 --> 00:16:43.300
have to manage some
of the infrastructure.

00:16:43.300 --> 00:16:45.880
Now it's a perfectly fine
solution, and that's fine.

00:16:45.880 --> 00:16:48.107
But when we look at
costs, you really

00:16:48.107 --> 00:16:50.440
have to understand that you're
going to be spending time

00:16:50.440 --> 00:16:52.398
spinning up instances,
and selecting instances,

00:16:52.398 --> 00:16:53.660
and scaling up instances.

00:16:53.660 --> 00:16:55.660
So the infrastructure
really is not abstracted.

00:16:55.660 --> 00:16:59.920
That abstraction has a
major impact on savings.

00:16:59.920 --> 00:17:01.974
So again, looking
back at the savings--

00:17:01.974 --> 00:17:04.390
and I'll leave it up there for
a little longer this time--

00:17:04.390 --> 00:17:06.369
first and foremost,
in the modeled

00:17:06.369 --> 00:17:08.650
organizations-- and this
is important to reiterate.

00:17:08.650 --> 00:17:11.901
That was for those three
modeled organizations.

00:17:11.901 --> 00:17:13.359
There are going to
be organizations

00:17:13.359 --> 00:17:15.220
that could potentially
have larger savings.

00:17:15.220 --> 00:17:16.784
There could be
organizations that

00:17:16.784 --> 00:17:17.950
may not have savings at all.

00:17:17.950 --> 00:17:19.300
And that's fair, and
Google knows that.

00:17:19.300 --> 00:17:19.841
We know that.

00:17:19.841 --> 00:17:21.910
Everybody knows that.

00:17:21.910 --> 00:17:24.310
But first and foremost, going
from on-prem to the cloud,

00:17:24.310 --> 00:17:25.810
you're going to save money.

00:17:25.810 --> 00:17:27.339
But what you're
going to see here

00:17:27.339 --> 00:17:32.190
is the cloud provider that
you select really matters.

00:17:32.190 --> 00:17:35.030
So if you're looking just at
this medium sized organization,

00:17:35.030 --> 00:17:37.670
great, I can save $300,000
going to Redshift.

00:17:37.670 --> 00:17:40.350
Well, I can save
another $1.7 million

00:17:40.350 --> 00:17:42.290
over three years
going to BigQuery,

00:17:42.290 --> 00:17:45.350
for again, this
modeled organization.

00:17:45.350 --> 00:17:49.070
So the point here
is one, you really

00:17:49.070 --> 00:17:53.000
need to understand your
costs to accurately model

00:17:53.000 --> 00:17:55.020
your specific organization.

00:17:55.020 --> 00:17:57.770
So some of the takeaways
from the validation first

00:17:57.770 --> 00:18:00.572
on the infrastructure
side, with BigQuery,

00:18:00.572 --> 00:18:02.030
you can leverage
on demand pricing,

00:18:02.030 --> 00:18:04.239
so there's no upfront
investment, which is fantastic.

00:18:04.239 --> 00:18:05.863
And then you really
don't have to worry

00:18:05.863 --> 00:18:07.730
about over-provisioning
resources,

00:18:07.730 --> 00:18:09.500
because they spin up
automatically, based

00:18:09.500 --> 00:18:11.810
on the demand of the query.

00:18:11.810 --> 00:18:15.080
Next on the OpEx side,
don't worry about sizing.

00:18:15.080 --> 00:18:17.840
Don't worry about
maintenance, really empowering

00:18:17.840 --> 00:18:20.510
that generalist to do more.

00:18:20.510 --> 00:18:22.754
And finally, cloud costs.

00:18:22.754 --> 00:18:24.170
A few things I
want to talk about.

00:18:24.170 --> 00:18:27.630
First, simplified pricing.

00:18:27.630 --> 00:18:29.110
It's all publicly available.

00:18:29.110 --> 00:18:29.940
You can see it.

00:18:29.940 --> 00:18:31.440
They pay for storage.

00:18:31.440 --> 00:18:35.430
You pay for processed data,
and then you pay for streaming.

00:18:35.430 --> 00:18:37.740
Now obviously,
that's for on-demand.

00:18:37.740 --> 00:18:40.570
Flat rate pricing is probably
similar to a reserved instance

00:18:40.570 --> 00:18:41.070
pricing.

00:18:41.070 --> 00:18:44.070
We can ask Sap later, but
I believe that's true.

00:18:44.070 --> 00:18:46.320
You don't have to worry
about scale and compute,

00:18:46.320 --> 00:18:48.060
because it's done automatically.

00:18:48.060 --> 00:18:49.800
And finally, the last piece.

00:18:49.800 --> 00:18:52.110
How many people have had
issues with interoperability

00:18:52.110 --> 00:18:54.360
when then they're deploying
a big data infrastructure?

00:18:54.360 --> 00:18:56.400
Anyone?

00:18:56.400 --> 00:18:57.725
OK, cool.

00:18:57.725 --> 00:18:59.100
That would have
been embarrassing

00:18:59.100 --> 00:19:01.330
if nobody raised their hand.

00:19:01.330 --> 00:19:03.240
But for the most
part, Google handles

00:19:03.240 --> 00:19:06.870
the interoperability side of
incorporating new technologies.

00:19:06.870 --> 00:19:09.289
So you pretty much-- you
keep leveraging the platform.

00:19:09.289 --> 00:19:10.830
You have no idea
that you're actually

00:19:10.830 --> 00:19:12.746
leveraging the latest
and greatest technology,

00:19:12.746 --> 00:19:15.550
because they take care of the
integration of that for you.

00:19:15.550 --> 00:19:18.695
So with that mind, I'm going
to hand it over to Sap and Sam.

00:19:18.695 --> 00:19:20.820
And Sam's going to share
some experiences he's had.

00:19:25.154 --> 00:19:26.570
SAPTARSHI MUKHERJEE:
Thanks, Mike.

00:19:26.570 --> 00:19:29.510
That's really helpful insight,
but let's make it real.

00:19:29.510 --> 00:19:34.430
So we have Sam King, and Sam
is an engineering manager

00:19:34.430 --> 00:19:35.490
in Nuna Health.

00:19:35.490 --> 00:19:38.000
They're a startup based
out of San Francisco.

00:19:38.000 --> 00:19:41.010
And do you want to quickly talk
about Nuna, what you guys do?

00:19:41.010 --> 00:19:43.460
And then we can jump into
the specific topic of moving

00:19:43.460 --> 00:19:45.470
data warehouse to BigQuery.

00:19:45.470 --> 00:19:47.810
SAM KING: Yeah, so
Nuna as a company

00:19:47.810 --> 00:19:50.900
tries to help folks understand
health data so that they

00:19:50.900 --> 00:19:53.210
can make better decisions,
folks like the government

00:19:53.210 --> 00:19:56.480
with Medicare and Medicaid,
as well as employer sponsored

00:19:56.480 --> 00:19:57.966
health plans and
insurance plans.

00:19:57.966 --> 00:19:59.090
SAPTARSHI MUKHERJEE: Great.

00:19:59.090 --> 00:20:00.923
And then you are dealing
with a lot of data.

00:20:00.923 --> 00:20:03.240
Every company is, no
exception, I'm sure.

00:20:03.240 --> 00:20:06.740
So before you moved to BigQuery,
what was the on-premise system

00:20:06.740 --> 00:20:08.270
that you were using?

00:20:08.270 --> 00:20:09.770
SAM KING: Yeah, so
we pretty quickly

00:20:09.770 --> 00:20:12.050
made the decision that we
wanted to be on the cloud.

00:20:12.050 --> 00:20:15.920
But our previous solution
before we were on BigQuery,

00:20:15.920 --> 00:20:18.200
we started out
with a open source

00:20:18.200 --> 00:20:20.120
distributed query engine.

00:20:20.120 --> 00:20:22.620
And this worked fine
to start out with,

00:20:22.620 --> 00:20:24.680
but once we were scaling
up, we started running

00:20:24.680 --> 00:20:26.780
into a lot of problems with it.

00:20:26.780 --> 00:20:29.690
Queries that used to take
seconds started taking minutes.

00:20:29.690 --> 00:20:32.300
There were a lot of errors
that we were encountering, even

00:20:32.300 --> 00:20:34.220
with pretty small queries.

00:20:34.220 --> 00:20:37.430
And also, in order to
deal with a lot of that,

00:20:37.430 --> 00:20:39.740
we needed to over-provision
the cluster, which

00:20:39.740 --> 00:20:41.810
resulted in a pretty high cost.

00:20:41.810 --> 00:20:44.420
And as a result, we
knew that we would

00:20:44.420 --> 00:20:46.129
be able to fix those
problems, but it

00:20:46.129 --> 00:20:47.420
would have taken a lot of time.

00:20:47.420 --> 00:20:51.287
So we started to look into
different alternatives.

00:20:51.287 --> 00:20:53.120
SAPTARSHI MUKHERJEE:
And then eventually you

00:20:53.120 --> 00:20:53.870
moved to BigQuery.

00:20:53.870 --> 00:20:56.900
So can you please take us
through what was your decision

00:20:56.900 --> 00:20:57.620
making criteria?

00:20:57.620 --> 00:21:00.170
What were the process
that you followed?

00:21:00.170 --> 00:21:03.080
SAM KING: Yeah, so at Nuna,
we're a data-driven company.

00:21:03.080 --> 00:21:05.390
We try to put a lot of
discipline between all

00:21:05.390 --> 00:21:06.830
of the decisions that we make.

00:21:06.830 --> 00:21:10.280
And so for this one, we
put together a spreadsheet.

00:21:10.280 --> 00:21:12.380
You can see a model
of it up there.

00:21:12.380 --> 00:21:15.230
And you can see BigQuery
is on the right.

00:21:15.230 --> 00:21:17.510
We also evaluated
BigQuery against a bunch

00:21:17.510 --> 00:21:21.290
of other different solutions,
including hosted ones, as well

00:21:21.290 --> 00:21:25.190
as self-hosted solutions,
similar to the status quo

00:21:25.190 --> 00:21:28.510
solution that we have
on the far left there.

00:21:28.510 --> 00:21:32.060
On each of the rows, you
can see a different thing

00:21:32.060 --> 00:21:33.180
that we care about.

00:21:33.180 --> 00:21:37.400
So you can see we really
care about stability,

00:21:37.400 --> 00:21:40.160
and so that's weighted
as a critical feature.

00:21:40.160 --> 00:21:43.010
And there are a bunch of
other ones that are listed.

00:21:43.010 --> 00:21:44.570
And you might be
wondering why we

00:21:44.570 --> 00:21:48.740
have some of these features
in a talk about cost.

00:21:48.740 --> 00:21:50.720
And part of this is
expanding the notion

00:21:50.720 --> 00:21:53.700
of what you think of as
total cost of ownership.

00:21:53.700 --> 00:21:57.290
So when we talk about stability,
for us, that really is a cost

00:21:57.290 --> 00:22:00.200
concern, because if we
miss our customer SLAs,

00:22:00.200 --> 00:22:01.940
then we're not getting paid.

00:22:01.940 --> 00:22:05.510
Same deal with things like
operational experience.

00:22:05.510 --> 00:22:07.580
If we already know
how to do something,

00:22:07.580 --> 00:22:11.780
then we don't need to invest
time and the human cost there

00:22:11.780 --> 00:22:14.840
in order to learn
about a new technology.

00:22:14.840 --> 00:22:18.740
And so we took all of these
things that we care about,

00:22:18.740 --> 00:22:21.710
we weighted them, and
then we filled out,

00:22:21.710 --> 00:22:25.130
how good is each of these
technologies in each

00:22:25.130 --> 00:22:26.880
of these categories.

00:22:26.880 --> 00:22:29.720
So you can see for our
status quo solution,

00:22:29.720 --> 00:22:33.260
it actually rates good in terms
of operational experience,

00:22:33.260 --> 00:22:36.890
because we already have
some experience using it.

00:22:36.890 --> 00:22:38.690
We knew what the problems were.

00:22:38.690 --> 00:22:41.240
Whereas with overall
stability, that

00:22:41.240 --> 00:22:43.430
rated poor because
of all of the errors

00:22:43.430 --> 00:22:45.920
that we were talking about.

00:22:45.920 --> 00:22:49.130
The next thing that we did after
we filled out a draft of this

00:22:49.130 --> 00:22:51.440
was reach out to a
bunch of other folks

00:22:51.440 --> 00:22:54.130
throughout the company to
get their opinion on this,

00:22:54.130 --> 00:22:56.210
so shared the spreadsheet.

00:22:56.210 --> 00:23:00.230
We got folks, including the
BI teams, including the CTO,

00:23:00.230 --> 00:23:03.500
including some non-technical
folks in the company who

00:23:03.500 --> 00:23:05.900
primarily care about
cost, to comment

00:23:05.900 --> 00:23:08.690
on each of these weightings,
on each of these values,

00:23:08.690 --> 00:23:10.760
so that the company
as a whole could

00:23:10.760 --> 00:23:13.817
come to an agreement in terms
of making this decision.

00:23:13.817 --> 00:23:15.650
And that was one of the
most important parts

00:23:15.650 --> 00:23:18.620
of this process,
because initially, there

00:23:18.620 --> 00:23:22.160
was a lot of resistance in the
company to going multi-cloud.

00:23:22.160 --> 00:23:25.130
But because the
decision process was

00:23:25.130 --> 00:23:27.170
so collaborative
and transparent,

00:23:27.170 --> 00:23:29.480
we were able to get the
buy-in that we needed.

00:23:29.480 --> 00:23:32.730
And you can see at
the very bottom there,

00:23:32.730 --> 00:23:34.130
there are some scores.

00:23:34.130 --> 00:23:37.280
So we used the
sidebar on the right,

00:23:37.280 --> 00:23:41.890
and we multiplied the weights
by each of the values,

00:23:41.890 --> 00:23:43.700
summed it up to come
to those scores.

00:23:43.700 --> 00:23:46.430
And then we did a
proof of concept

00:23:46.430 --> 00:23:50.130
with BigQuery as well as
hosted solution number one.

00:23:50.130 --> 00:23:52.580
And when we did that,
we found that BigQuery

00:23:52.580 --> 00:23:56.870
was even easier than we
had predicted to prototype

00:23:56.870 --> 00:24:01.365
and to get running, so we
switched over to using that.

00:24:01.365 --> 00:24:02.450
SAPTARSHI MUKHERJEE: OK.

00:24:02.450 --> 00:24:04.370
And Nuna deals with a
lot of health care data.

00:24:04.370 --> 00:24:06.740
Like you mentioned, you are in
the business of health care, so

00:24:06.740 --> 00:24:08.270
regulatory compliance
and security,

00:24:08.270 --> 00:24:11.870
those are important
consideration.

00:24:11.870 --> 00:24:14.736
How do you evaluate those
options in the cloud?

00:24:14.736 --> 00:24:15.860
SAM KING: Yeah, absolutely.

00:24:15.860 --> 00:24:18.950
At Nuna, because we deal
with health care data,

00:24:18.950 --> 00:24:21.350
we have a saying, every
row of the data is a life.

00:24:21.350 --> 00:24:23.830
And so privacy and
security for that data

00:24:23.830 --> 00:24:25.990
is absolutely a top concern.

00:24:25.990 --> 00:24:31.530
And for us, one of the
biggest features of BigQuery

00:24:31.530 --> 00:24:34.470
and Google Cloud Platform when
we were making this decision

00:24:34.470 --> 00:24:38.070
was projects within
Google Cloud Platform.

00:24:38.070 --> 00:24:41.730
So a project is
basically full separation

00:24:41.730 --> 00:24:44.730
of all of the resources,
including BigQuery,

00:24:44.730 --> 00:24:47.670
as well as Google Cloud
Storage and any other service

00:24:47.670 --> 00:24:48.720
that you use.

00:24:48.720 --> 00:24:51.750
And at Nuna, we care a
lot about data separation,

00:24:51.750 --> 00:24:55.800
because we need to keep
each of our clients' data

00:24:55.800 --> 00:24:57.510
separate from one another.

00:24:57.510 --> 00:25:00.450
With our other cloud, we had
to write a lot of custom code

00:25:00.450 --> 00:25:02.070
in order to achieve that.

00:25:02.070 --> 00:25:04.530
And that's pretty
expensive to maintain,

00:25:04.530 --> 00:25:08.190
because not only if there
were a security breach

00:25:08.190 --> 00:25:10.650
would that be very
costly, but the amount

00:25:10.650 --> 00:25:12.630
of time that it takes
us to understand

00:25:12.630 --> 00:25:14.550
all of multi-tenancy
support that we've

00:25:14.550 --> 00:25:18.210
built in just to develop
those technologies

00:25:18.210 --> 00:25:19.660
are pretty significant.

00:25:19.660 --> 00:25:23.670
And so with BigQuery, we
just used separate projects,

00:25:23.670 --> 00:25:27.880
and that works pretty
transparently for us.

00:25:27.880 --> 00:25:30.660
And in addition to that,
it helps improve the cost

00:25:30.660 --> 00:25:34.576
transparency and the cost
controls that we have.

00:25:34.576 --> 00:25:35.700
SAPTARSHI MUKHERJEE: Right.

00:25:35.700 --> 00:25:37.283
And what has been
experience thus far?

00:25:37.283 --> 00:25:40.050
You have been in BigQuery
for couple of years now?

00:25:40.050 --> 00:25:41.820
SAM KING: Yeah, we've
been using BigQuery

00:25:41.820 --> 00:25:43.510
for a little over a year.

00:25:43.510 --> 00:25:46.200
And so our experience
has been very positive.

00:25:46.200 --> 00:25:49.020
Both in terms of human costs
as well as machine costs,

00:25:49.020 --> 00:25:52.140
we've seen cost reductions
when moving to BigQuery.

00:25:52.140 --> 00:25:54.180
From the human cost
perspective, if we

00:25:54.180 --> 00:25:56.520
had stayed with that
open source technology,

00:25:56.520 --> 00:25:58.140
we would have needed
to invest time

00:25:58.140 --> 00:26:02.220
in improving the scalability,
reducing latency, reducing

00:26:02.220 --> 00:26:05.500
error rates, and improving
multi-tenancy support.

00:26:05.500 --> 00:26:08.190
And each of those things would
have taken multiple engineer

00:26:08.190 --> 00:26:09.000
months.

00:26:09.000 --> 00:26:13.500
And as a startup, we really
didn't have that time,

00:26:13.500 --> 00:26:17.520
even if that were a
good use of human time.

00:26:17.520 --> 00:26:21.750
And with BigQuery
on the other hand,

00:26:21.750 --> 00:26:23.280
it only took us
a couple of weeks

00:26:23.280 --> 00:26:26.280
to build out the infrastructure
and automation to switch over,

00:26:26.280 --> 00:26:28.200
and another couple
of weeks to move over

00:26:28.200 --> 00:26:30.180
our SQL to the new dialect.

00:26:30.180 --> 00:26:33.010
And since then, it's
been pretty hands off.

00:26:33.010 --> 00:26:37.230
So from a human perspective,
the costs have been much lower.

00:26:37.230 --> 00:26:39.670
And then from a
machine perspective,

00:26:39.670 --> 00:26:43.380
it's been an even bigger
win, since, as Sap mentioned

00:26:43.380 --> 00:26:49.260
earlier, the separation between
storage and compute on BigQuery

00:26:49.260 --> 00:26:50.940
has been big for
us, since we have

00:26:50.940 --> 00:26:52.980
a lot of data that
we need to store

00:26:52.980 --> 00:26:55.080
but that we don't
query very often.

00:26:55.080 --> 00:26:58.350
And in addition to
that, we have a lot

00:26:58.350 --> 00:27:01.410
of customers that need to have
access to all of their data

00:27:01.410 --> 00:27:03.840
within a few seconds, but
they might only query it once

00:27:03.840 --> 00:27:04.590
a week.

00:27:04.590 --> 00:27:06.960
And so because of that,
we need to support

00:27:06.960 --> 00:27:09.210
very bursty query patterns.

00:27:09.210 --> 00:27:11.446
And BigQuery's been
great about that.

00:27:11.446 --> 00:27:13.320
And the only way that
we were able to support

00:27:13.320 --> 00:27:15.420
that with our open
source solution

00:27:15.420 --> 00:27:17.940
was by dramatically
over-provisioning the cluster.

00:27:17.940 --> 00:27:20.370
So switching to BigQuery
has reduced the cost for us

00:27:20.370 --> 00:27:21.324
all around.

00:27:21.324 --> 00:27:22.740
SAPTARSHI MUKHERJEE:
Great, great.

00:27:22.740 --> 00:27:24.362
Thanks for sharing
the insights, Sam.

00:27:24.362 --> 00:27:25.320
Thanks for coming over.

00:27:25.320 --> 00:27:26.800
Really appreciate it.

00:27:26.800 --> 00:27:28.270
Thank you.

00:27:28.270 --> 00:27:29.580
So moving on.

00:27:29.580 --> 00:27:31.170
Talking about the
use case too, I

00:27:31.170 --> 00:27:33.870
think the neat summary from
the BigQuery conversation

00:27:33.870 --> 00:27:37.680
is not all the data warehouses
that are running in the cloud

00:27:37.680 --> 00:27:38.490
are the same.

00:27:38.490 --> 00:27:41.730
We need to be really careful
to understand how those data

00:27:41.730 --> 00:27:44.114
warehouses are designed,
how they are architected,

00:27:44.114 --> 00:27:45.780
how it's going to
support your business,

00:27:45.780 --> 00:27:49.180
your very specific use case,
your requirements, to come

00:27:49.180 --> 00:27:50.520
to a TCO model.

00:27:50.520 --> 00:27:52.860
The second one is moving
Hadoop and Spark to cloud.

00:27:52.860 --> 00:27:57.300
Again, how many of you are using
some form of Hadoop or Spark

00:27:57.300 --> 00:27:59.130
for data processing?

00:27:59.130 --> 00:27:59.820
A lot of you.

00:27:59.820 --> 00:28:00.390
Great.

00:28:00.390 --> 00:28:02.490
We all love open source.

00:28:02.490 --> 00:28:04.500
Google Cloud is
committed to innovate.

00:28:04.500 --> 00:28:06.510
And while we
innovate, we're also

00:28:06.510 --> 00:28:08.670
embracing the best of
the Apache open source

00:28:08.670 --> 00:28:10.140
project for data processing.

00:28:10.140 --> 00:28:12.630
Dataproc is a fully
managed service

00:28:12.630 --> 00:28:15.600
that runs on our cloud for
Apache, Hadoop, and Spark.

00:28:15.600 --> 00:28:18.600
We just announced the support
for Apache workflow for--

00:28:18.600 --> 00:28:21.660
Apache Airflow for
workflow orchestrations.

00:28:21.660 --> 00:28:24.240
But let's talk about
this particular use case.

00:28:24.240 --> 00:28:27.855
So this is a typical
lifecycle of Hadoop

00:28:27.855 --> 00:28:29.910
and Spark cluster
implementation, right.

00:28:29.910 --> 00:28:33.090
So you first obtain the hardware
server, configure the server,

00:28:33.090 --> 00:28:36.150
install the OSS, then go
through multiple iteration

00:28:36.150 --> 00:28:39.030
of reconfiguration,
optimization, debugging,

00:28:39.030 --> 00:28:41.470
and then finally
process the data.

00:28:41.470 --> 00:28:44.100
It's a pretty labor
intensive process.

00:28:44.100 --> 00:28:46.500
And we are working
with companies, really

00:28:46.500 --> 00:28:49.050
large, planet-scale
internet companies

00:28:49.050 --> 00:28:50.330
like Yahoos of the world.

00:28:50.330 --> 00:28:51.829
Twitter just announced
that they are

00:28:51.829 --> 00:28:53.850
going to move thousands
of Hadoop clusters

00:28:53.850 --> 00:28:55.470
to Google Cloud.

00:28:55.470 --> 00:28:57.360
We are working with
disruptive startups

00:28:57.360 --> 00:29:00.180
who are very leanly
resourced but managing

00:29:00.180 --> 00:29:01.457
a huge amount of data.

00:29:01.457 --> 00:29:03.540
And then we are also working
with large enterprise

00:29:03.540 --> 00:29:05.415
companies, like the Home
Depots of the world,

00:29:05.415 --> 00:29:07.310
the HSBCs is of the
world, and they're

00:29:07.310 --> 00:29:10.230
moving their big data processing
workloads to Google Cloud.

00:29:10.230 --> 00:29:12.044
There is a reason behind it.

00:29:12.044 --> 00:29:16.540
So when we get to
conversations with customers,

00:29:16.540 --> 00:29:19.430
and we typically ask, so you're
using your Hadoop and Spark

00:29:19.430 --> 00:29:22.216
solutions on-premise
or in some other cloud.

00:29:22.216 --> 00:29:24.340
What are some of the core
challenges that you face?

00:29:24.340 --> 00:29:26.560
I think scaling is a
major, major problem.

00:29:26.560 --> 00:29:29.380
Especially with spiky workloads
with lot of seasonality

00:29:29.380 --> 00:29:32.170
involved, where the data
volume could be unpredictable,

00:29:32.170 --> 00:29:34.365
scaling often chokes
customer down,

00:29:34.365 --> 00:29:35.740
because they have
pre-provisioned

00:29:35.740 --> 00:29:39.730
capacity and expanding that is
going to take a lot of time.

00:29:39.730 --> 00:29:41.980
Utilization, and
this is the flip side

00:29:41.980 --> 00:29:43.510
of over-provisioning--
like if you

00:29:43.510 --> 00:29:46.810
have to provision for
the most spiky workload,

00:29:46.810 --> 00:29:49.940
then you end up creating on an
under-utilized infrastructure,

00:29:49.940 --> 00:29:52.370
for you have to be
accountable to your CFO.

00:29:52.370 --> 00:29:55.310
Hey, my resource utilization
is less than 20%.

00:29:55.310 --> 00:29:57.580
Why should I keep continuing
this in my data center?

00:29:57.580 --> 00:30:00.640
And then if you try to
optimize either of these two,

00:30:00.640 --> 00:30:02.530
you encounter a
lot of cost pain.

00:30:02.530 --> 00:30:05.829
Like if you keep changing
your cluster configuration,

00:30:05.829 --> 00:30:07.870
you'll have to keep
re-provisioning your hardware

00:30:07.870 --> 00:30:09.050
infrastructure.

00:30:09.050 --> 00:30:10.932
There's a lot of cost
associated with it,

00:30:10.932 --> 00:30:13.390
not only just hardware cost,
but professional services that

00:30:13.390 --> 00:30:15.110
comes along with it.

00:30:15.110 --> 00:30:17.770
So what Google Cloud
offers in this space

00:30:17.770 --> 00:30:21.010
is Dataproc, which is a fully
managed Hadoop and Spark

00:30:21.010 --> 00:30:25.030
service that allows you
to run Hadoop and Spark

00:30:25.030 --> 00:30:27.880
jobs on our cloud.

00:30:27.880 --> 00:30:31.140
And Dataproc is an
interesting service.

00:30:31.140 --> 00:30:34.080
So there are a couple of options
for companies to run Hadoop

00:30:34.080 --> 00:30:35.650
and Spark jobs on Google Cloud.

00:30:35.650 --> 00:30:38.200
One is if you're
running it on-premise,

00:30:38.200 --> 00:30:41.191
you pretty much do everything
for running a data processing

00:30:41.191 --> 00:30:41.690
job.

00:30:41.690 --> 00:30:43.030
You write custom code.

00:30:43.030 --> 00:30:47.470
You do monitoring of the
health of the cluster, scaling,

00:30:47.470 --> 00:30:49.780
job submission,
connectivity, deployment,

00:30:49.780 --> 00:30:51.180
everything pretty much.

00:30:51.180 --> 00:30:55.750
Then if you choose to move your
Hadoop and Spark job as a VM

00:30:55.750 --> 00:30:59.590
option on GCE, Google Compute
Engine in Google Cloud,

00:30:59.590 --> 00:31:02.050
you still do a
bit of automation.

00:31:02.050 --> 00:31:03.914
Like connectivity,
deployment, creation,

00:31:03.914 --> 00:31:05.830
those steps are taken
care of by Google Cloud,

00:31:05.830 --> 00:31:09.230
but the rest of the thing, you
still have to do as a company.

00:31:09.230 --> 00:31:11.860
And then when you move to
Dataproc, it's fully managed.

00:31:11.860 --> 00:31:13.360
So almost the only
thing that you do

00:31:13.360 --> 00:31:14.730
is just writing custom codes.

00:31:14.730 --> 00:31:17.630
The rest of the things are
taken care of by the platform.

00:31:17.630 --> 00:31:19.261
And by the way, small plug-in.

00:31:19.261 --> 00:31:21.760
We're going to have a really
interesting announcement around

00:31:21.760 --> 00:31:25.310
Dataproc on Wednesday--
that's tomorrow--

00:31:25.310 --> 00:31:26.590
that I can't talk now.

00:31:26.590 --> 00:31:28.660
I wish I could.

00:31:28.660 --> 00:31:32.560
But we had ESG, and
they came again just

00:31:32.560 --> 00:31:35.924
to prove the same point,
that not all Hadoop and Spark

00:31:35.924 --> 00:31:37.840
managed services that
are running in the cloud

00:31:37.840 --> 00:31:38.690
are the same.

00:31:38.690 --> 00:31:40.750
So how is Dataproc
different from some

00:31:40.750 --> 00:31:43.420
of the other alternates
that are out in the market?

00:31:43.420 --> 00:31:45.850
And if a customer is already
running it in on-premise,

00:31:45.850 --> 00:31:47.997
why should they even
bother to move to cloud?

00:31:47.997 --> 00:31:50.080
So Mike, do you want to
share some of the insights

00:31:50.080 --> 00:31:50.871
from that research?

00:31:54.760 --> 00:31:59.990
MIKE LEONE: So when evaluating
TCO for Hadoop, Spark,

00:31:59.990 --> 00:32:03.640
there's a couple of obvious
considerations, right, node

00:32:03.640 --> 00:32:04.140
count.

00:32:04.140 --> 00:32:06.360
How many nodes are you
going to have in a cluster?

00:32:06.360 --> 00:32:07.859
What are your
resource requirements?

00:32:07.859 --> 00:32:10.230
What's your workload demand?

00:32:10.230 --> 00:32:12.810
Those are two pretty
obvious costs.

00:32:12.810 --> 00:32:16.170
Now we start to get to
tricky areas, daily runtime.

00:32:16.170 --> 00:32:20.170
Is your existing cluster running
24/7, or is there downtime?

00:32:20.170 --> 00:32:22.740
And if there's downtime,
how much is it down?

00:32:22.740 --> 00:32:25.660
Are you running batch
requests during evening hours

00:32:25.660 --> 00:32:28.320
and then during the day, or
allowing for ad hoc querying

00:32:28.320 --> 00:32:30.600
while you're doing
batch at the same time?

00:32:30.600 --> 00:32:35.580
And with that in mind,
the ad hoc querying

00:32:35.580 --> 00:32:38.400
starts factoring in that
IT administration time.

00:32:38.400 --> 00:32:43.660
So is your IT administrator, or
are you, kind of a gatekeeper?

00:32:43.660 --> 00:32:46.230
Or are you trying
to prevent analysts

00:32:46.230 --> 00:32:49.050
from running ad hoc queries,
because they could potentially

00:32:49.050 --> 00:32:54.360
disrupt batch queries that
are going on almost 24/7

00:32:54.360 --> 00:32:55.510
to some degree?

00:32:55.510 --> 00:32:57.600
So you really have to
factor in those costs,

00:32:57.600 --> 00:33:01.410
and you have to understand
the types of runtimes

00:33:01.410 --> 00:33:03.130
that your environment's
working with.

00:33:03.130 --> 00:33:04.980
And finally, burst requirements.

00:33:04.980 --> 00:33:07.230
Sap mentioned this.

00:33:07.230 --> 00:33:09.010
You have to plan for
over-provisioning

00:33:09.010 --> 00:33:10.740
when you're on-prem.

00:33:10.740 --> 00:33:13.085
With a cloud, it's not
necessarily something

00:33:13.085 --> 00:33:15.460
you have to think about,
because you can easily scale up.

00:33:15.460 --> 00:33:16.543
You can easily scale down.

00:33:16.543 --> 00:33:19.050
That elasticity that
the cloud provides

00:33:19.050 --> 00:33:23.680
enables you to say to save
costs when there is downtime.

00:33:23.680 --> 00:33:26.972
So we've leveraged the
same buckets as before,

00:33:26.972 --> 00:33:28.680
but we broke it down
a little differently

00:33:28.680 --> 00:33:33.972
to account for Hadoop
and Spark to some degree.

00:33:33.972 --> 00:33:35.430
And we broke out
server and storage

00:33:35.430 --> 00:33:38.110
separate, because
it's decoupled.

00:33:38.110 --> 00:33:39.600
We also factored
in infrastructure.

00:33:39.600 --> 00:33:43.500
Infrastructure here happens
to be specifically associated

00:33:43.500 --> 00:33:46.800
with power cooling, floor
space, that kind of stuff.

00:33:46.800 --> 00:33:49.920
And then, finally administration
and those cloud costs.

00:33:49.920 --> 00:33:52.660
So takeaway away one,
you're going to save money,

00:33:52.660 --> 00:33:55.470
regardless of the cloud provider
you use for the organization

00:33:55.470 --> 00:33:57.390
that we modeled here.

00:33:57.390 --> 00:34:01.650
Key takeaways, save
up to 70% or more

00:34:01.650 --> 00:34:04.320
on the reduction in hardware.

00:34:04.320 --> 00:34:06.427
That's a big savings.

00:34:06.427 --> 00:34:08.010
OpEx side of things,
you could save up

00:34:08.010 --> 00:34:11.040
to 85% going from
on-prem to the cloud,

00:34:11.040 --> 00:34:13.239
based on what we modeled here.

00:34:13.239 --> 00:34:17.560
And some of the key points
around EMR versus Dataproc,

00:34:17.560 --> 00:34:21.330
in this case, you're going to
see similar areas of savings

00:34:21.330 --> 00:34:25.170
that we saw with BigQuery around
administration, around cloud.

00:34:25.170 --> 00:34:29.960
It's not as drastic, I would
say, as the BigQuery side,

00:34:29.960 --> 00:34:32.969
but you're still going to
gain an economic advantage

00:34:32.969 --> 00:34:35.190
by leveraging
BigQuery in this case.

00:34:35.190 --> 00:34:38.489
Now the last piece I want to
mention is at the bottom here.

00:34:38.489 --> 00:34:41.350
It's agility, and this goes
to the opportunity costs.

00:34:41.350 --> 00:34:43.500
So when we were
talking to customers

00:34:43.500 --> 00:34:46.530
and interviewing them
as part of this study,

00:34:46.530 --> 00:34:49.739
these are quotes from
three different customers,

00:34:49.739 --> 00:34:51.960
across three different
verticals, unrelated,

00:34:51.960 --> 00:34:56.880
that I thought was interesting,
that, again, we can't quantify.

00:34:56.880 --> 00:34:59.552
But there's definitely a
cost associated with it.

00:34:59.552 --> 00:35:01.260
So first is around
being able to leverage

00:35:01.260 --> 00:35:04.500
a platform that will allow you
to increase current revenue

00:35:04.500 --> 00:35:05.019
streams.

00:35:05.019 --> 00:35:06.810
If there's a business
demand for something,

00:35:06.810 --> 00:35:09.300
you can easily access
it in the cloud,

00:35:09.300 --> 00:35:12.990
and you can satisfy the
requirements of that workload.

00:35:12.990 --> 00:35:16.020
Next is being able to
capitalize on a new opportunity

00:35:16.020 --> 00:35:17.850
and being able to,
again, leverage

00:35:17.850 --> 00:35:20.520
that scalable system
that can instantaneously

00:35:20.520 --> 00:35:22.500
cater to your workload demands.

00:35:22.500 --> 00:35:24.946
And finally, the
last piece-- and I

00:35:24.946 --> 00:35:26.070
found this one interesting.

00:35:26.070 --> 00:35:27.679
I've heard this
multiple times so far,

00:35:27.679 --> 00:35:29.220
over the course of
the last two days,

00:35:29.220 --> 00:35:31.560
from customers who
leverage not just Dataproc,

00:35:31.560 --> 00:35:35.040
but a number of GCP services--

00:35:35.040 --> 00:35:40.326
the ability to succeed, but
more importantly, fail faster.

00:35:40.326 --> 00:35:43.710
And I want to go on
this for just a second.

00:35:43.710 --> 00:35:45.210
If you're leveraging
a cloud service

00:35:45.210 --> 00:35:47.070
and you're able
to provide access

00:35:47.070 --> 00:35:48.890
to more people in
your organization,

00:35:48.890 --> 00:35:52.260
you're going to obviously be
able to gain insights faster.

00:35:52.260 --> 00:35:55.059
And with that in mind,
you'll understand

00:35:55.059 --> 00:35:56.850
if you're going to be
able to continue down

00:35:56.850 --> 00:35:58.590
the path of an initiative
you have, or you can just

00:35:58.590 --> 00:35:59.790
stop and say, you
know what, this

00:35:59.790 --> 00:36:00.660
isn't going to be successful.

00:36:00.660 --> 00:36:01.985
Let's move on to the next one.

00:36:01.985 --> 00:36:04.110
That is something that this
particular organization

00:36:04.110 --> 00:36:05.730
was able to do multiple times.

00:36:05.730 --> 00:36:08.430
And they really emphasized
the fail faster.

00:36:08.430 --> 00:36:12.019
So with that in mind, I'm
going to shift back to Sap,

00:36:12.019 --> 00:36:14.310
and he's going to highlight
a couple of other customers

00:36:14.310 --> 00:36:16.980
that weren't part of
our study to highlight

00:36:16.980 --> 00:36:21.494
a couple of other quantitative
or qualitative savings.

00:36:21.494 --> 00:36:22.910
SAPTARSHI MUKHERJEE:
Thanks, Mike.

00:36:22.910 --> 00:36:27.140
So just to kind of rephrase
some of these concepts

00:36:27.140 --> 00:36:30.340
and make it real, we
took two examples, one,

00:36:30.340 --> 00:36:34.340
a traditional enterprise, very
traditional, but really great

00:36:34.340 --> 00:36:35.780
organization, Telegraph Group.

00:36:35.780 --> 00:36:37.790
They run one of the
most popular newspapers

00:36:37.790 --> 00:36:41.045
in UK, more than [? 19 ?]
million subscribers.

00:36:41.045 --> 00:36:45.230
They were facing challenges to
understand customer sentiment

00:36:45.230 --> 00:36:48.530
and do personalization,
because their existing systems

00:36:48.530 --> 00:36:50.650
that they had for
their data warehouse

00:36:50.650 --> 00:36:53.526
were not able to scale
for the type of analysis

00:36:53.526 --> 00:36:54.650
that they are trying to do.

00:36:54.650 --> 00:36:56.066
So what they did
was they at first

00:36:56.066 --> 00:37:00.320
tried another cloud
hosted Hadoop offering,

00:37:00.320 --> 00:37:02.690
just to see if they can
offload that particular part

00:37:02.690 --> 00:37:04.790
of the workload and
run there, and continue

00:37:04.790 --> 00:37:06.920
to run their on-premise
data warehouse system.

00:37:06.920 --> 00:37:08.180
It was just not scalable.

00:37:08.180 --> 00:37:10.010
The systems were not
talking to each other.

00:37:10.010 --> 00:37:11.880
The cost was going
through the roof.

00:37:11.880 --> 00:37:14.450
So they finally started
doing a POC with BigQuery

00:37:14.450 --> 00:37:16.130
and then started using it.

00:37:16.130 --> 00:37:18.290
Now Telegraph Media
is essentially

00:37:18.290 --> 00:37:22.460
analyzing more than 4 terabyte
of data in less than a minute.

00:37:22.460 --> 00:37:24.350
They minimized the
cost per query.

00:37:24.350 --> 00:37:27.320
Look at how fine grained some
of these customers are going.

00:37:27.320 --> 00:37:30.130
They're essentially trying to
track how many times the data

00:37:30.130 --> 00:37:31.940
warehouse is getting
queried, what's

00:37:31.940 --> 00:37:34.400
the volume of data that
is getting queried, what's

00:37:34.400 --> 00:37:35.810
the cost per query.

00:37:35.810 --> 00:37:37.669
And they are seeing
cost per query saving.

00:37:37.669 --> 00:37:39.710
That's like the ultimate
realization of the value

00:37:39.710 --> 00:37:41.070
from a data warehouse.

00:37:41.070 --> 00:37:44.570
And then it reduces their
overall data analysis time

00:37:44.570 --> 00:37:46.490
from hours to minutes.

00:37:46.490 --> 00:37:48.710
And it's making the whole
organization much more

00:37:48.710 --> 00:37:49.640
responsive.

00:37:49.640 --> 00:37:52.940
They're able to launch campaigns
for their customers much more

00:37:52.940 --> 00:37:55.520
responsively, rather than
waiting for days or weeks

00:37:55.520 --> 00:37:58.366
for IT teams to get back
to them with results.

00:37:58.366 --> 00:37:59.990
And then the other
example that we took

00:37:59.990 --> 00:38:01.260
is a disruptive startup.

00:38:01.260 --> 00:38:05.480
So Ocado is one of the largest
online retailers based out

00:38:05.480 --> 00:38:09.080
of UK, and they are
redefining grocery experience

00:38:09.080 --> 00:38:12.140
through online web and
mobile application.

00:38:12.140 --> 00:38:15.279
And Ocado, you can
read about Ocado.

00:38:15.279 --> 00:38:16.820
There are multiple
articles coming in

00:38:16.820 --> 00:38:19.670
"Economist," like how they're
disrupting the whole industry.

00:38:19.670 --> 00:38:21.590
They are one of our
BigQuery customers.

00:38:21.590 --> 00:38:25.130
And Ocado is dealing with
massive, massive amount

00:38:25.130 --> 00:38:27.840
of data, hundreds of
terabytes of data.

00:38:27.840 --> 00:38:29.940
And they had two concerns.

00:38:29.940 --> 00:38:33.410
One is scale, because they
are moving so fast, growing

00:38:33.410 --> 00:38:35.660
so fast, they wanted the
data analytics infrastructure

00:38:35.660 --> 00:38:37.010
to support that growth.

00:38:37.010 --> 00:38:38.960
And the second thing
is, while they grow

00:38:38.960 --> 00:38:40.760
and while they
become responsive,

00:38:40.760 --> 00:38:43.570
they can't have unprecedented
cost exposure on their data

00:38:43.570 --> 00:38:44.510
analytics side.

00:38:44.510 --> 00:38:45.909
So they came to Google Cloud.

00:38:45.909 --> 00:38:47.450
They're not only
just using BigQuery,

00:38:47.450 --> 00:38:49.900
but they are using a
whole other set of tools,

00:38:49.900 --> 00:38:52.880
using Dataproc, GCS.

00:38:52.880 --> 00:38:55.910
And what they got is
one, query performance--

00:38:55.910 --> 00:38:58.530
it improved dramatically
after moving to BigQuery, so

00:38:58.530 --> 00:39:00.590
faster time to insight.

00:39:00.590 --> 00:39:02.890
It increased their
contact center efficiency.

00:39:02.890 --> 00:39:05.390
They're using not only our data
analytics stack, but machine

00:39:05.390 --> 00:39:08.840
learning to do labeling for
emails, automatic responses

00:39:08.840 --> 00:39:10.350
back to their customers.

00:39:10.350 --> 00:39:12.560
And Mike talked about
opportunity cost.

00:39:12.560 --> 00:39:15.830
This is how the company is
realizing opportunity cost,

00:39:15.830 --> 00:39:19.220
by introducing inventory
churn, by being more responsive

00:39:19.220 --> 00:39:21.170
and reaching out to
the customers on time

00:39:21.170 --> 00:39:23.000
and saving lost sales.

00:39:23.000 --> 00:39:25.610
And you have to start
quantifying those numbers

00:39:25.610 --> 00:39:28.690
by factoring in
opportunity cost.

00:39:28.690 --> 00:39:31.750
So we talked a lot
about different concepts

00:39:31.750 --> 00:39:33.970
and frameworks,
gave you use cases.

00:39:33.970 --> 00:39:34.590
We had Nuna.

00:39:34.590 --> 00:39:36.890
We talked about customers.

00:39:36.890 --> 00:39:38.620
So how do you take it
back home and start

00:39:38.620 --> 00:39:40.120
applying for your businesses?

00:39:40.120 --> 00:39:42.680
So a few things if
you have to remember--

00:39:42.680 --> 00:39:43.840
I think to summarize--

00:39:43.840 --> 00:39:45.160
I mean, we are having
a conversation right

00:39:45.160 --> 00:39:46.360
before this presentation.

00:39:46.360 --> 00:39:49.120
If I have to summarize this,
we call it TL;DR in Google,

00:39:49.120 --> 00:39:51.070
Too Long; Do Read.

00:39:51.070 --> 00:39:52.991
So basically, all that
glitters is not gold.

00:39:52.991 --> 00:39:54.990
All the data warehouses
are running in the cloud

00:39:54.990 --> 00:39:56.990
were not designed for cloud.

00:39:56.990 --> 00:39:59.590
So if you are
evaluating options,

00:39:59.590 --> 00:40:02.830
start thinking about a long
term horizon, not just what's

00:40:02.830 --> 00:40:06.947
the upfront cost of
option A versus B.

00:40:06.947 --> 00:40:09.280
It could be a one year, two
year, or three year horizon,

00:40:09.280 --> 00:40:12.730
based on your cost appetite
and exposure appetite.

00:40:12.730 --> 00:40:15.100
The second thing
is go beyond CapEx.

00:40:15.100 --> 00:40:17.830
Just don't think about, OK, how
much I have already invested,

00:40:17.830 --> 00:40:21.010
what's my sunk cost, what's my
CapEx going to be, and how much

00:40:21.010 --> 00:40:22.270
my OpEx going to be.

00:40:22.270 --> 00:40:24.490
It's not a CapEx
versus OpEx comparison.

00:40:24.490 --> 00:40:26.200
We need to factor
in other costs.

00:40:26.200 --> 00:40:27.680
Build a framework.

00:40:27.680 --> 00:40:30.350
Nuna showed how
they went granular.

00:40:30.350 --> 00:40:32.950
They built a framework
that was pretty exhaustive,

00:40:32.950 --> 00:40:36.190
that actually was designed for
their specific business use

00:40:36.190 --> 00:40:36.702
case.

00:40:36.702 --> 00:40:39.160
So for your business, you have
to build your own framework.

00:40:39.160 --> 00:40:41.240
Factor in business priorities.

00:40:41.240 --> 00:40:42.550
Scope in data growth.

00:40:42.550 --> 00:40:45.192
The amount of data that
you have in your system

00:40:45.192 --> 00:40:47.650
is not going to remain the same
over the course of next two

00:40:47.650 --> 00:40:49.870
or three years, and that's
going to be the number one

00:40:49.870 --> 00:40:52.480
blocker for you to grow.

00:40:52.480 --> 00:40:54.430
User consideration
and usage pattern.

00:40:54.430 --> 00:40:56.020
How many users are BI user?

00:40:56.020 --> 00:40:58.450
How many users are
doing ad hoc analysis?

00:40:58.450 --> 00:41:02.200
How many of them are doing data
science or machine learning

00:41:02.200 --> 00:41:03.130
workflows?

00:41:03.130 --> 00:41:04.270
And go granular.

00:41:04.270 --> 00:41:07.750
Specificity and granularity
is very important in cost

00:41:07.750 --> 00:41:08.782
consideration.

00:41:08.782 --> 00:41:10.240
And then consider
opportunity cost.

00:41:10.240 --> 00:41:13.220
Like Ocado, for example,
or Telegraph Media,

00:41:13.220 --> 00:41:16.030
they're all thinking about,
how is my data management

00:41:16.030 --> 00:41:18.940
platform going to support
my business vision and goal?

00:41:18.940 --> 00:41:22.000
And then compare cost
with next viable options.

00:41:22.000 --> 00:41:24.680
Like hey, BigQuery cost
x amount of dollars.

00:41:24.680 --> 00:41:25.900
It's just not enough.

00:41:25.900 --> 00:41:28.450
If it is not BigQuery,
if it is Redshift

00:41:28.450 --> 00:41:30.382
or if it is my
on-premise Hadoop system,

00:41:30.382 --> 00:41:32.590
what are some of the next
viable options do you have?

00:41:32.590 --> 00:41:35.230
And then do a comparative
study across those options

00:41:35.230 --> 00:41:37.250
and then pick your best bet.

00:41:37.250 --> 00:41:38.950
So with that, I
think we have come

00:41:38.950 --> 00:41:41.200
to the conclusion of this
particular presentation.

00:41:41.200 --> 00:41:44.470
I hope you got
something that's new.

00:41:44.470 --> 00:41:46.120
We refreshed some
of the memories

00:41:46.120 --> 00:41:47.800
for sure, like
taking about BigQuery

00:41:47.800 --> 00:41:50.130
for those who are already
using it, or Dataproc.

00:41:50.130 --> 00:41:53.260
We'll be here staying out
to take questions and have

00:41:53.260 --> 00:41:54.460
conversations with you.

00:41:54.460 --> 00:41:57.130
I really want to
thank Sam from Nuna

00:41:57.130 --> 00:41:59.947
and then Mike from ESG
for their participation

00:41:59.947 --> 00:42:01.780
and thank all of you
for your presence here.

00:42:01.780 --> 00:42:02.655
Really appreciate it.

00:42:02.655 --> 00:42:03.610
Thank you.

00:42:03.610 --> 00:42:07.260
